//
// D-K2's SUBSYSTEM DEPENDENCY RULE, IN ONE PLACE (P3b/P4b R-5).
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. The rule "bit X requires bit Y" was stated in SIX places, and the audit found
// two of them WRONG in the same direction:
//
//   * MG_Pipe/MGPipe.h's comment said "THREE OF THEM HAVE A DEPENDENCY" (there are six rows) and
//     "The mirror pairs (10 without 11, 10 without 9, 7 without 10) are all fine" - but 10
//     without 11 is exactly D-K2's FOURTH row, refused by the server at
//     Managers.cpp ResolveTextureResourceSubsystemArm and withheld by the client at
//     PipeFill.cpp's table. PipeFill.cpp says so out loud: "The brief's original
//     'bit 10 without 11 is fine' is WITHDRAWN for P4a as built.";
//   * MG_Backend/DirectGLES/Managers.h's header comment repeats both defects, in the header of
//     the file that IMPLEMENTS the refusal.
//
// Neither wrong comment could fail anything, which is the whole argument for a table: the two
// EXECUTABLE statements of the rule are in different link units with no shared header, and the
// four PROSE statements drifted because nothing compared them to anything.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS. The rule, once, as rows. It is a preprocessor-only table like
// MG_Remote/FatalFamilies.def - no generator reads it - and it carries pure bit constants, so
// including it from MGPipe.h keeps check_include_closure.py's value-header rule satisfied.
//
// WHO READS IT. MGPipe.h turns it into kMGPipeSubsystemDependencies[] and
// MGPipeSubsystemRequires(). The two executable statements of the rule are:
//
//   CLIENT dependency check   MG_Impl/Pipe/PipeFill.cpp's kMGPipeP4aFamilyDependencies /
//                             P4aFamilyDependenciesAreSet (bits 9-12), plus P5eFamilyIsLive's
//                             hand-coded bit-13 row.
//   SERVER consumer gate      MG_Backend/DirectGLES/Managers.cpp's four
//                             Resolve<Family>SubsystemArm() over PipeSubsystemDependencyMissing
//                             (bits 9-12), plus ResolveVertexInputSubsystemArm (bit 8) and
//                             DirectGLES.cpp's ResolveBufferBindingSubsystemArm (bit 13).
//
// THEY ARE NOT SWITCHED OVER IN THIS COMMIT, deliberately: PipeFill.cpp is the contract
// package's file for the whole phase (TextureEmit.h states that rule) and Managers.cpp belongs
// to wave 2-D package D1 while it is in flight. The anti-drift property does not wait for that,
// because MG_Test/Backend/DirectGLES/SubsystemDepsTest.cpp drives BOTH readers at every
// interesting mask and compares them against these rows - a change to either side alone reds.
// Switching the two readers to read this table is two one-line changes for the integrator.
//
// THE ROWS ARE NOT TRANSITIVE, and that is load-bearing rather than an oversight: each reader
// tests the required BITS against MOBILEGL_PIPE_PUSH alone, never the other family's liveness,
// because Espryt's resolvers classify their arms from that mask alone. Making the rows
// transitive here would make the two sides disagree at some mask, which is the failure the
// table exists to prevent. PipeFill.cpp:1576-1581 argues this at length and
// TextureEmitTest.cpp:1278-1283 pins it.
//
// THE MIRROR PAIRS THAT STAY FINE are stated in MGPipe.h beside the table rather than here,
// because an absence is not a row.

// clang-format off

// X(Family, Requires, Why)
//   Family   - exactly one kMGPipeSubsystem* bit
//   Requires - the bits MOBILEGL_PIPE_PUSH must ALSO carry for that family to be live; 0 is a
//              ROW and not an absence, so the table covers its families exhaustively
#define MGL_SUBSYSTEM_DEPENDENCY_LIST(X) \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemVertexInput,      kMGPipeSubsystemResources,                                  "a vertex buffer binding names a Buffer handle and only bit 7 puts one in the slot table (P3a's shipped shape; ResolveVertexInputSubsystemArm)") \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemFramebuffer,      kMGPipeSubsystemTextureResources,                           "every MGPSurface::Res names a Texture or Renderbuffer handle and only bit 10 populates those two slot tables (ResolveFramebufferSubsystemArm)") \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemTextureResources, kMGPipeSubsystemResources | kMGPipeSubsystemSamplers,       "a buffer texture's BufferForTexBuffer names a Buffer handle (bit 7); and MGPTextureParams::BuiltinSampler is a SamplerCso HANDLE only bit 11 mints, whose null the applier treats as Fatal{ProtocolCorruption} rather than a decline - D-K2's fourth row, ID-14/ID-15, which WITHDRAWS the brief's 'bit 10 without 11 is fine'") \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemSamplers,         kMGPipeSubsystemTextureResources,                           "every MGPBoundView::Texture and MGPImageView::Res names a Texture handle and only bit 10 populates that slot table; with the row above this is SYMMETRIC - bits 10 and 11 are one arm with two switches (ResolveSamplerSubsystemArm)") \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemPrograms,         0,                                                          "a ShaderCso handle names no texture and no buffer, the archive rides beside the record as a companion pointer, and the server's extra specialisation inputs come from state the backend already holds (ResolveProgramSubsystemArm)") \
    X(kMGPipeSubsystemBufferBindings,   kMGPipeSubsystemResources,                                  "every MGPBufferRange::Res names a Buffer handle and only bit 7 puts one in the slot table, so without it every server-side EnsureBufferResourceForHandle would mint a twin with no record behind it (P5e sb; ResolveBufferBindingSubsystemArm)")

// clang-format on
